::CW_U05_L07_Q01::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A writer chooses a portfolio piece because feedback helped improve its ending. What does this show?{
~A. Random selection.#Incorrect. The choice is tied to growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A reason to avoid revision.#Incorrect. Feedback led to revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A format error.#Incorrect. The issue is selection rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. A strong selection rationale.#Correct. It explains why the piece belongs in the portfolio.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q02::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which should happen before final polishing?{
~A. Fix only spelling.#Incorrect. Polishing comes after larger revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Check purpose, feedback, structure, and format needs.#Correct. Larger revision decisions should come first. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Write the final note before reading the draft.#Incorrect. The note should match the final piece.
~D. Submit the rough draft.#Incorrect. Rough drafts are not automatically portfolio-ready.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which is the strongest revision evidence?{
~A. A statement that the draft is typed.#Incorrect. Typing is not revision evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A random color choice.#Incorrect. This does not show revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. A before-and-after sentence showing how feedback led to clearer imagery.#Correct. This proves a meaningful change. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. A note that no changes were made.#Incorrect. That is not revision evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q04::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A poem has strong imagery but confusing line breaks. Which portfolio preparation step fits best?{
=A. Revise the line breaks so the format supports meaning and pacing.#Correct. This addresses format-specific quality. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Ignore the line breaks.#Incorrect. Format affects reader experience. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Delete the poem automatically.#Incorrect. The poem may be revised. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add a summary of every image.#Incorrect. Overexplaining does not fix format. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q05::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which portfolio note sentence is weakest?{
~A. I selected this because feedback helped me clarify the ending.#Incorrect. This gives a strong reason. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I chose this because I like it.#Correct. This is vague and does not explain readiness.
~C. I revised the final image so the reader could infer the speaker's choice.#Incorrect. This explains craft effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The final version is ready because the format and conventions are checked.#Incorrect. This explains readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q06::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A story's dialogue is unclear because speakers change in one paragraph. What should be checked?{
~A. Whether every sentence can be deleted.#Incorrect. Deleting everything is not targeted. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Whether the title is random.#Incorrect. The problem is dialogue format. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Whether the story should ignore readers.#Incorrect. Reader clarity matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Paragraph breaks and dialogue tags.#Correct. These help readers track speakers. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q07::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which statement best separates revision from polishing?{
~A. Revision only fixes spelling.#Incorrect. That is polishing. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Polishing should happen before purpose is clear.#Incorrect. Purpose comes first. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Revision improves meaning or reader effect; polishing checks final details.#Correct. This accurately separates the two steps. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Revision means changing words randomly.#Incorrect. Revision is purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q08::[MLA.CW.REV.01] What should a writer do if feedback conflicts with the piece's purpose?{
=A. Consider it, then choose the revision that protects the intended effect.#Correct. This uses feedback thoughtfully. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Accept it automatically.#Incorrect. Feedback should be evaluated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Reject all future feedback.#Incorrect. Other feedback may be useful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove the piece from the portfolio immediately.#Incorrect. A conflict does not automatically disqualify the piece.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q09::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which piece is most portfolio-ready?{
~A. A rough draft with no review.#Incorrect. It lacks readiness checks. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A revised piece with feedback evidence, checked format, and a clear portfolio note.#Correct. This includes the major readiness components. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. A piece selected only because it is short.#Incorrect. Selection needs a stronger rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A typed draft with random spacing.#Incorrect. Random spacing does not prove readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q10::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which question should guide final readiness?{
~A. Is it the first file in the folder?#Incorrect. File order does not prove readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Did I avoid all feedback?#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Does the final version show purpose, revision, format control, and reader readiness?#Correct. This checks the full portfolio standard. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Is it longer than every other piece?#Incorrect. Length does not prove quality. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q11::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A writer adds one sensory image after feedback that the setting felt empty. What type of change is this?{
~A. Random decoration.#Incorrect. The change responds to feedback. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A reason to skip format checks.#Incorrect. Format still matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A title only.#Incorrect. The change is within the piece. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. A meaningful revision.#Correct. It improves setting detail based on feedback.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q12::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which response best explains final polish?{
~A. Changing the whole purpose at the last minute.#Incorrect. That is not polish. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Checking conventions, consistency, and formatting after major revision is complete.#Correct. Polish comes after larger revisions. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Ignoring punctuation.#Incorrect. Conventions are part of polish. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Selecting pieces randomly.#Incorrect. Selection is a separate step. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q13::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which portfolio preparation step should come first?{
=A. Select a piece with a reason.#Correct. Selection begins the preparation plan. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Write a final note before choosing a piece.#Incorrect. The note depends on the selected piece.
~C. Submit before reading feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Delete all drafts.#Incorrect. Drafts may provide revision evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q14::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A portfolio note says, "Feedback showed my ending was sudden." What should come next?{
~A. A statement that no revision happened.#Incorrect. The note should explain the response. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. An unrelated font choice.#Incorrect. The issue is the ending. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. An explanation of what was revised and how the ending improved.#Correct. This completes the reflection. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. A personal attack on the reviewer.#Incorrect. The response should stay craft-focused. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q15::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Why should the final format match the creative form?{
~A. Format never matters.#Incorrect. Format changes reader experience. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Format replaces revision evidence.#Incorrect. Both are needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Format should be random.#Incorrect. Format choices should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Format affects how readers follow structure, speakers, pacing, and meaning.#Correct. Format is part of publication quality. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q16::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which action weakens portfolio readiness?{
~A. Checking conventions after revision.#Incorrect. This strengthens readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Ignoring a confusing ending because the spelling is correct.#Correct. Surface correctness does not solve meaning problems.
~C. Explaining a revision decision.#Incorrect. This strengthens the portfolio note. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Checking genre format.#Incorrect. This supports publication quality. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q17::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which statement best shows author growth?{
=A. I used feedback to replace direct explanation with an image that lets the reader infer emotion.#Correct. This shows craft growth. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. I did not revise anything.#Incorrect. This does not show growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I selected the first thing I found.#Incorrect. This does not show intentional selection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I changed the title randomly.#Incorrect. Random change is not growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q18::[MLA.CW.REV.01] What is the purpose of a selection rationale?{
~A. To replace the creative piece.#Incorrect. It supports, not replaces, the piece. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. To list every typo.#Incorrect. That is not selection rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. To explain why the piece belongs in the portfolio.#Correct. It connects the choice to growth or readiness.
~D. To avoid revision.#Incorrect. It should often reference revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q19::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which revision should come before an author note?{
~A. Write a note that hides the confusion.#Incorrect. A note should not cover unresolved problems.
~B. Submit immediately.#Incorrect. Readiness checks are still needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Ignore reader confusion.#Incorrect. Reader understanding matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Clarify the unclear speaker in the final scene.#Correct. The piece should be revised before the note explains it.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q20::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A writer's portfolio note explains purpose, feedback, revision, and readiness. What does this demonstrate?{
~A. It demonstrates random selection.#Incorrect. The explanation is purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. It demonstrates portfolio readiness reasoning.#Correct. It explains why the piece is ready.
~C. It demonstrates no revision.#Incorrect. It names revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It demonstrates that format does not matter.#Incorrect. Readiness includes format. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q21::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which answer best handles a draft with strong meaning but weak conventions?{
~A. Delete the meaning.#Incorrect. The meaning is a strength. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Ignore conventions entirely.#Incorrect. Conventions affect readability. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Preserve the meaning and complete a focused convention check before final submission.#Correct. This improves polish without damaging purpose. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Change the genre randomly.#Incorrect. Genre change is not needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q22::[MLA.CW.REV.01] What makes a before-and-after example useful?{
=A. It shows exactly what changed and why the revision improved the piece.#Correct. This is strong revision evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. It hides all changes.#Incorrect. Evidence should be visible. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It proves first drafts are always final.#Incorrect. It shows revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It replaces the final piece.#Incorrect. It supports the final piece. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q23::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which final checkpoint belongs in Lesson 7?{
~A. Private information collection.#Incorrect. That is not part of portfolio readiness.
~B. A random list of favorite colors.#Incorrect. That does not address writing readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A refusal to revise.#Incorrect. Revision is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. A portfolio-ready plan with selection rationale, revision evidence, final checklist, and note.#Correct. This matches the lesson checkpoint. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q24::[MLA.CW.REV.01] A piece is polished but the writer cannot explain why it belongs in the portfolio. What is missing?{
=A. A selection rationale.#Correct. The writer needs to justify inclusion. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. A random new draft.#Incorrect. The issue is explanation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. More spelling errors.#Incorrect. Errors would weaken readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A missing title only.#Incorrect. The missing piece is rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L07_Q25::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which response best summarizes Lesson 7 mastery?{
~A. I typed a draft and stopped.#Incorrect. That is not portfolio-ready. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I selected a piece intentionally, revised from evidence, checked format and conventions, and wrote a portfolio note explaining readiness.#Correct. This includes the core mastery components. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. I accepted every suggestion automatically.#Incorrect. Feedback must be evaluated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I chose a piece with no reason.#Incorrect. Selection needs a rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
